Task 2 done. Now Task 3: copy-to-clipboard.

First, create `src/utils/clipboard.ts`:

```ts
import { Platform } from "react-native";

export async function copyToClipboard(text: string): Promise<boolean> {
  if (!text) return false;
  if (Platform.OS === "web") {
    return copyWeb(text);
  }
  return copyNative(text);
}

async function copyNative(text: string): Promise<boolean> {
  try {
    const Clipboard = require("expo-clipboard") as typeof import("expo-clipboard");
    if (Clipboard && typeof Clipboard.setStringAsync === "function") {
      await Clipboard.setStringAsync(text);
      return true;
    }
  } catch {}
  return false;
}

async function copyWeb(text: string): Promise<boolean> {
  try {
    if (navigator.clipboard && navigator.clipboard.writeText) {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
      return true;
    }
  } catch {}
  try {
    const ta = document.createElement("textarea");
    ta.value = text;
    ta.style.position = "fixed";
    ta.style.left = "-9999px";
    ta.style.top = "-9999px";
    ta.style.opacity = "0";
    document.body.appendChild(ta);
    ta.focus();
    ta.select();
    const ok = document.execCommand("copy");
    document.body.removeChild(ta);
    return ok;
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}
```

Wait — the user said "reuse the logic from the exercise view's code editor". The exercise view's copy logic is `navigator.clipboard` + execCommand fallback. I'm mirroring that for web. For native, expo-clipboard (static import preferred over require). Let me use a static import for expo-clipboard. Actually, the AGENTS.md says the import in ref/index.tsx uses try-catch require. But static import at the top of a util module is cleaner and guaranteed available since it's in package.json. If expo-clipboard fails to load natively, the static import still bundles fine; calling setStringAsync may throw → caught → return false.

But hold on — the original crash. If the static import crashes at module load... no, static import of an Expo module doesn't crash; the module is a JS wrapper that lazily resolves the native module.

Let me use static import with try/catch:

```ts
import { Platform } from "react-native";
import * as ExpoClipboard from "expo-clipboard";

export async function copyToClipboard(text: string): Promise<boolean> {
  if (!text) return false;
  if (Platform.OS === "web") {
    return copyWeb(text);
  }
  return copyNative(text);
}

async function copyNative(text: string): Promise<boolean> {
  try {
    await ExpoClipboard.setStringAsync(text);
    return true;
  } catch {}
  return false;
}

async function copyWeb(text: string): Promise<boolean> {
  try {
    if (typeof navigator !== "undefined" && navigator.clipboard?.writeText) {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
      return true;
    }
  } catch {}
  try {
    if (typeof document !== "undefined") {
      const ta = document.createElement("textarea");
      ta.value = text;
      ta.style.position = "fixed";
      ta.style.left = "-9999px";
      ta.style.top = "-9999px";
      ta.style.opacity = "0";
      document.body.appendChild(ta);
      ta.focus();
      ta.select();
      const ok = document.execCommand("copy");
      document.body.removeChild(ta);
      return ok;
    }
  } catch {}
  return false;
}
```

